Z||000000||firstfiero||06-02-2001||10:55 AM||||       I just got the book Pontiac fiero performance portfolio and now have a few questions? I alway here the mr2 guys talking that the 1st gen. supercharged models were faster then all fieros but that's not true in the test against a formula it most certanly is but then it downed on me The numbers they had for that formula were alot slower then the fastback. So I went back to the article befor it to look at the numbers for a fastback of the same year and sure enough the fastback was faster then formula and also faster then the supercharged mr2. Why is the formula slower then the fastback....aero dynamics??  I thought they were the same car with a different skin.   <P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif">         <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||TRiAD||06-02-2001||11:00 AM||||Are you talking 0-60 ot top end?<BR>0-60 would have to be gearing or tranny (make sure none of the cars tested is an Auto), top end would also be possibly gearing, but more likely ECU programming (limiter or not) and aerodynamis. <P>BTW, the old SC MR2 was NOT that fast. A current Civic with a D16Z6 (SOHC VTEC, like the EX Coupe) could take it. <P>Oh, and post the numbers, man!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||saleenfiero||06-02-2001||11:01 AM||||Different magazines testing different cars in different conditions at different altitudes account for variations like this I have found.  As for hard #'s, I'm not sure. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Boxdin||06-02-2001||11:04 AM||||At our local road course race track, I was in my 88 Formula Automatic, and my buddy was in his blown MR2 (lowered,and all that crap) My car had 98k miles, and was tired, and I kept up with him lap after lap until his car broke. He was angry and amazed !<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||firstfiero||06-02-2001||11:24 AM||||okay here goes...these cars are all from 87<P>gt fastback 5 speed manual<BR>0 to 60    7.53 sec<BR>quarter   15.25 sec<P>fiero formula 5 speed<BR>0 to 60 8.0<BR>quarter 16.0<P>mr2 supercharged 5 speed<BR>0 to 60 7.0<BR>quarter 15.3<P>sorry guys...look like it is faster by just a tick to 60....but we got it in the quarter. Looks like it would come down to is the better driver.<BR>  <BR> <P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif">         <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||06-02-2001||12:00 PM||||Differences in cars, weather, drivers, conditions and track locations etc etc will create lots of differences.  A difference like that between a GT and a Formula is likely to be factor other than the car itself.  Both use the same drivetrains, and the Formula's tend to be a little lighter.  <P>Those numbers are all close enogh that any of the three would win a race if they had the best driver.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||06-02-2001||01:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>okay here goes...these cars are all from 87<P>fiero formula 5 speed<BR>0 to 60 8.0<BR>quarter 16.0<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Um, you're book's in error.  There were no Formulas in 87.  It was an 88 only model.  And all things being equal, the Formula should be slightly faster than the GT, but even differences between two identical cars could account for some variance in performance.  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||88formula||06-02-2001||02:35 PM||||I think the 88 cars are a little heavier than the earlier years because of the redesigned suspension. Performance not only varies because of track conditions and the driver but also between two of the same cars. You could have two formulas with the same options and one might put out a little more power than the other because of GM's loose manufacturing tolerances.<P>The MR2 is smaller and lighter than the Formula and therefore more nimble. The Formula has higher lateral grip than the 88 MR2, better brakes and it’s safer in a crash. There is also more room in the Fiero. The only MR2 I like is the second generation turbocharger models. In the Brooklands Book I have they compare the MR2 to the Fiero Formula. The MR2 won most of the comparison tests. In the article they say in everyday driving they would rather live with the easy to drive Formula than the MR2. Don’t forget the MR2 cost something like $3500 more than the formula that year. For the Formula to come that close in performance and even beat the MR2 in lateral grip is very good for a car that costs $3500 less.     <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
